WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.150
Andy Galpin: What do we actually know

00:00:03.149 --> 00:00:06.209
about sleep differences
between men and women?

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.929
What I actually want
to know about is chromosomally.

00:00:10.419 --> 00:00:12.720
When you tease out the hormones,

00:00:12.965 --> 00:00:17.519
whether it's a polymorphism
or some other activity

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:19.500
at the level of the genome,

00:00:20.039 --> 00:00:23.239
how is that actually manipulating,
manifesting itself into sleep?

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:27.320
Any idea what mechanisms would possibly be
there if it's not endocrine?

00:00:27.578 --> 00:00:32.073
Allison Brager: In terms
of mechanism of action,

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:35.369
we know, again,
there's differences in sleep pressure.

00:00:35.369 --> 00:00:37.799
If you unpack the onion,

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:42.569
sleep pressure is manifest
from increasing levels

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:46.889
of extracellular adenosine production

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:49.409
within the brain,
particularly the basal forebrain,

00:00:49.439 --> 00:00:51.030
which is a wake-promoting area.

00:00:52.649 --> 00:00:55.320
If sleep pressure is accumulating quickly,

00:00:56.159 --> 00:00:59.730
which seems to be the case
in biological males

00:00:59.909 --> 00:01:03.000
by way of the testes-determining gene,

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.779
then there's more rapid accumulation
of extracellular adenosine,

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:11.409
and the only way to pay off
that increasing sleep pressure

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:13.469
is through daytime sleep.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:20.369
With females, it just essentially means
the rate of accumulation

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:23.240
of sleep pressure is lower,

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:27.809
which means that the production
of extracellular adenosine

00:01:27.929 --> 00:01:28.929
would be lower.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.810
Now, whether that's been studied,

00:01:31.739 --> 00:01:35.040
it hasn't, but hypothetically,
just knowing

00:01:35.369 --> 00:01:41.038
with all the myriad of studies
around accumulating sleep pressure

00:01:41.039 --> 00:01:42.683
and extracellular adenosine,

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.974
we predict that's
the mechanism of action.

00:01:45.975 --> 00:01:47.175
Andy: That's the closest

00:01:47.246 --> 00:01:49.340
we can come at this point, right?
Allison: Right.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:51.060
Andy: Is it fair?
Is it a trope?

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.298
I certainly know
that it feels like the guys

00:01:54.299 --> 00:01:56.349
that I know versus the girls that I know,

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:00.447
guys fall asleep fast.

00:02:00.448 --> 00:02:01.448
Allison: Oh, yes.

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:03.710
Andy: Is that actually been demonstrated?
Do we know that?

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:05.610
Allison: Yes. That's been
demonstrated, again,

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:08.819
in these preclinical
transgenic mouse models

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:10.650
using the four-core genotype.

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:13.799
It's also been demonstrated
in human studies.

00:02:14.579 --> 00:02:17.729
In those studies that were done
in the '80s by Terry Lee

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:18.869
and Rachel Manber,

00:02:19.109 --> 00:02:22.980
that's exactly what they found
is sleep onset for men

00:02:23.039 --> 00:02:24.210
is much quicker.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.830
Then when you look at time
to restorative sleep,

00:02:29.009 --> 00:02:33.479
so that entry into stage 3 non-REM
and then REM sleep,

00:02:35.009 --> 00:02:36.922
the latency is quicker for females.

00:02:37.559 --> 00:02:41.399
It might take them longer to fall asleep,
but to get that restorative sleep,

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:44.399
it's much quicker
and then much more efficient

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:45.193
across the night.

00:02:45.303 --> 00:02:49.208
Andy: Do we have any teleological
or evolutionary guess as to why

00:02:49.478 --> 00:02:50.478
that setup is there?

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.867
Allison: I think it all goes
back to child-rearing, right?

00:02:53.917 --> 00:02:56.217
Who's responsible for children?

00:02:57.179 --> 00:02:59.669
Men are responsible
for propagating children,

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:01.050
but women,

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:02.939
they are the caretakers.

00:03:03.029 --> 00:03:06.009
I think these are these
built-in neurochemical,

00:03:06.059 --> 00:03:11.849
neuroanatomical circuits
that exist because of relationships

00:03:11.879 --> 00:03:14.079
between who actually
takes care of the child.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:15.539
Andy: How much of it is,

00:03:15.539 --> 00:03:17.739
if you know, psychology
versus biology there?

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:20.090
The classic thing
you'll hear is like,

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:22.229
"Oh, women ruminate more.
They think about stuff more.

00:03:22.229 --> 00:03:26.638
They're paying attention,"
or is it simply any host of cascades

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:27.929
haven't kicked off yet?

00:03:28.147 --> 00:03:29.280
The pressure isn't high enough.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:30.730
Do we have any idea of what--

00:03:31.147 --> 00:03:33.089
I'm sure it's both are contributing.

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:36.730
Allison: I think
that's the psychological aspect,

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:42.581
from my lens,
is a part of that biological blueprint.

00:03:42.631 --> 00:03:47.699
It's because females are going
to have greater vigilance

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:49.450
across the day.

00:03:49.469 --> 00:03:51.899
What neurochemically is vigilance?

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.412
It's increased acetylcholine release

00:03:54.413 --> 00:03:58.589
and activation
of acetylcholine areas of the brain,

00:03:58.590 --> 00:03:59.880
like the basal forebrain,

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:02.490
that are going to lead to that.

00:04:03.569 --> 00:04:08.250
Again, I see the world
from the lens of nature versus nurture.

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:13.711
I'm not a very good colleague
in departments of psychology.

00:04:13.741 --> 00:04:15.779
Andy: You have a degree in psychology.

00:04:16.379 --> 00:04:18.720
Allison: I have
an undergraduate degree in psychology,

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.220
but really-
Andy: Neuroscience.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:21.541
Allison: -it's all neuroscience.

00:04:21.541 --> 00:04:28.978
I was fortunate enough that I did go
to a university that really thrived

00:04:28.979 --> 00:04:30.540
on its neuroscience curriculum.

00:04:31.159 --> 00:04:32.970
Andy: Nonetheless,
I heard you're a psychologist,

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:35.163
and what I just heard you
say was psychology

00:04:35.164 --> 00:04:36.784
is just misunderstood physiology.

00:04:36.784 --> 00:04:38.114
I get it.

00:04:38.144 --> 00:04:39.344
That's what you said.
Allison: I agree.

00:04:39.344 --> 00:04:40.579
Yes, it is. [laughs]
Andy: Dr. Brager said it.

00:04:40.579 --> 00:04:41.679
Hold my stamp to that.

00:04:42.792 --> 00:04:43.682
Acetylcholine,

00:04:43.683 --> 00:04:48.030
is that a total concentration issue
that generally is higher,

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:50.320
or is it a receptor density,
or is it both?

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:55.587
Do we have any idea of what are actually
the sex differences between acetylcholine?

00:04:55.588 --> 00:04:57.188
Allison: That's a good question.

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:03.599
The studies that I know exist
on sex differences

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:07.386
focus on one particular area,
which is the basal forebrain.

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:12.023
That is the seat
of major acetylcholinergic production

00:05:12.073 --> 00:05:14.480
and release and activation
within the brain.

00:05:14.531 --> 00:05:18.959
Then there's cascading
effects on other areas.

00:05:21.659 --> 00:05:25.740
No, I actually think this is
the land of great unknown.

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:29.340
There haven't been a lot of studies
on sex differences

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:31.040
and physiology and behavior.

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:32.840
If there were, they were done again

00:05:32.872 --> 00:05:34.936
in the '80s and '90s-
Andy: Totally.

00:05:35.456 --> 00:05:37.386
Allison: You got me curious
about going back

00:05:37.386 --> 00:05:41.640
onto PubMed and Google Scholar
and looking this up.

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:44.322
Andy: I mean, the stuff
that has been done since then,

00:05:44.322 --> 00:05:46.610
a lot of it is questionnaires.

00:05:46.611 --> 00:05:47.205
It is.

00:05:47.235 --> 00:05:51.239
It's pretty low-fidelity stuff,
which is good and helpful.

00:05:51.259 --> 00:05:52.759
Allison: You need self-report.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:55.830
With our sleep studies
that we do in humans now,

00:05:56.099 --> 00:05:58.579
we always have
an objective endpoint endocrine

00:05:58.629 --> 00:06:05.640
or even behavioral cognitive measure,
but it's always paired with self-report.

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:07.739
Sometimes the self-report data

00:06:08.279 --> 00:06:11.040
is what lends
the more interesting findings.

00:06:11.369 --> 00:06:14.309
Andy: Women, men fall asleep differently.

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:17.160
We get that. What about total duration
and waking-up time?

00:06:17.309 --> 00:06:19.409
Any sex differences
that are notable there?

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:25.798
Allison: I'm happy you asked
that because I feel like that data

00:06:25.799 --> 00:06:31.859
has been taken a little bit too far
on social media that women need

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:32.909
more sleep than men.

00:06:33.869 --> 00:06:38.729
There is a difference in total sleep time
between men and women that was found again

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:43.739
in these studies in the 1980s,
and it's about 25 minutes

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:45.410
to 30 minutes different.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:49.980
I come from this lens,
is that clinically significant?

00:06:51.269 --> 00:06:54.059
I can't answer that,
but I feel like in social media

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:59.489
that people have taken that information
and have run with it that women

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:00.490
need to spend--

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:04.290
I think I saw something recently
like because women take

00:07:04.291 --> 00:07:06.089
this long to fall asleep,

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:12.000
they need to be in bed for nine
and a half hours versus a guy

00:07:12.001 --> 00:07:15.750
who falls asleep quickly,
they only need to be in bed for eight.

00:07:15.869 --> 00:07:19.770
I think, at a certain point,
that's just going to create

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:25.440
anxiety around the act of sleeping
that it's going to be more harmful

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:26.470
than helpful.

00:07:27.149 --> 00:07:29.939
Andy: I assume then also,
based on this men nap more

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:31.570
than women do.

00:07:31.592 --> 00:07:32.822
Allison: Yes, we know that.

00:07:32.822 --> 00:07:37.732
We know that experimentally,
we know that anecdotally,

00:07:38.082 --> 00:07:42.592
you just look at cultures around
the world that have siestas

00:07:42.913 --> 00:07:46.459
and/or it's adopted

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:49.738
and celebrated that people nap
during the day,

00:07:49.739 --> 00:07:51.269
as we know that men

00:07:52.049 --> 00:07:53.969
in general across cultures

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:59.100
are more prone to daytime sleepiness
and transgenic mice too.

00:07:59.339 --> 00:08:01.380
Andy: Yes, name there.

00:08:01.969 --> 00:08:05.419
Are people napping more or less
or the same than we have historically?

00:08:05.613 --> 00:08:07.043
Allison: That, I don't know.

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:08.830
I feel like,

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:12.270
at least in the last 10 years,

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:17.790
that stigma around napping
has been reduced,

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:18.820
I would say.

00:08:19.439 --> 00:08:21.386
It still exists in the military.
Andy: Of course.

00:08:21.386 --> 00:08:22.616
Allison: I don't think it's going away.

00:08:22.616 --> 00:08:25.925
Actually, my colleagues
and I from Walter Reed,

00:08:25.926 --> 00:08:30.556
we have a paper that we published
in the flagship journal of sleep around--

00:08:30.606 --> 00:08:32.820
it's called The Stigma
of Workplace Napping.

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:39.269
We strategically anonymously took quotes
from senior leaders in the military,

00:08:39.299 --> 00:08:41.128
and we were flabbergasted.

00:08:41.129 --> 00:08:43.440
They were like, "No, if my employees nap,

00:08:43.499 --> 00:08:44.499
they're lazy.

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:45.870
They're not productive."

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:47.080
I'm like,

00:08:47.839 --> 00:08:48.839
"Okay.

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:49.840
Just whatever."

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:51.150
[chuckles]

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:54.029
Andy: If you enjoyed this clip,
you can watch the full episode

00:08:54.029 --> 00:08:55.560
by clicking here.

00:08:56.079 --> 00:09:02.238
[music]

